WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
Let's walk through instruction decomposition and constraint design, breaking complex instructions

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.680
into ordered steps, defining scope, encoding format and length and tone constraints,

3
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.200
knowing when to split one instruction into more, and testing whether an instruction is specific

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.639
enough to be reliable. These are mechanics we're covering in this lesson. Let's jump into it.

5
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.959
A prompt that asks for everything at once usually delivers mediocre work on all of it.

6
00:00:28.959 --> 00:00:34.720
Instruction decomposition is the fix. You break a complex task into a sequence of simpler,

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.400
ordered sub-instructions, each with a single clear goal, which reduces ambiguity and makes

8
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.400
the output much easier to verify at every stage. Scope definition is the practice of explicitly

9
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.639
naming what to include, what to exclude, and what to assume. It closes the most common

10
00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:56.639
interpretation gaps without forcing you to specify every detail of the output.

11
00:00:57.279 --> 00:01:01.520
Constraint encoding is the translation of quality requirements into specific

12
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.800
instruction parameters, and each parameter closes a different category of interpretation gap.

13
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.720
Why does decomposition matter? When a single instruction contains multiple goals,

14
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:19.279
the model has to balance them simultaneously. That competition reduces the quality of every

15
00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:25.839
individual outcome. A task split into clear sequential steps consistently produces better

16
00:01:25.839 --> 00:01:31.440
results than the same task crammed into one large prompt, and it's also much easier to

17
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.800
manage and review. Each step can be checked independently before the next one runs.

18
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.400
If a step produces unexpected output, you know exactly where the problem occurred

19
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:49.040
and can fix it there. That precision is impossible when everything happens in a single call.

20
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:56.400
Decomposition converts one large, hard-to-debug prompt into a series of smaller, verifiable steps.

21
00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:03.120
Ordered decomposition means assigning each phase of a task its own instruction, with a clear

22
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.360
sequence between them. For a three-phase task, step 1 might extract the relevant information

23
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:16.320
from the source. Step 2 summarizes the extracted content according to a specific format. Step 3

24
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:24.000
reviews the summary for gaps or inconsistencies. Each step has one goal, one expected output type,

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.479
and one criterion you can use to verify it. When phase 1 completes and you check the extraction,

26
00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:37.759
you know the input to phase 2 is sound before you run it. That sequencing is what makes complex tasks

27
00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:44.559
tractable. You're solving three simple problems in order, not one complicated problem all at once.

28
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.880
Scope definition is one of the most underused tools in instruction writing. Most interpretation

29
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.800
gaps come from things that weren't said, rather than things that were said badly. Scope definition

30
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.360
addresses that directly, by forcing you to name the edges of the task explicitly. What should the

31
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.360
output include? List those elements by name. What should it exclude? State that directly, rather

32
00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:14.880
than hoping the model infers it. What can the model treat as already established context?

33
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.199
Name those assumptions so the model doesn't hedge or re-explain background you've already covered.

34
00:03:21.199 --> 00:03:27.279
And where does the task end? A clear stopping point prevents the model from continuing past

35
00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:33.759
the intended output. Defining all four of those boundaries turns an open-ended task into a closed

36
00:03:33.759 --> 00:03:40.160
one. The comparison between a single complex instruction and a decomposed one is worth making

37
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:47.039
concrete. Consider a contract analysis task. A single instruction might say, analyze this contract,

38
00:03:47.039 --> 00:03:53.279
identify the key risks, summarize each party's obligations, and flag any clauses that conflict

39
00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:59.199
with standard practice. That's four distinct goals in one call. The model will attempt all four,

40
00:03:59.199 --> 00:04:04.160
but each one gets reduced attention, and the outputs are harder to check individually.

41
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.559
Decomposed, the same task becomes three separate calls. List every obligation per party, assess

42
00:04:10.559 --> 00:04:17.279
each obligation as standard or unusual, then summarize only the unusual ones. Each step has

43
00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:23.600
one goal. The output of each step feeds into the next. Every step is verifiable on its own.

44
00:04:24.559 --> 00:04:29.600
Constraints are parameters that narrow the output space without changing what the core task is

45
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:36.480
asking for. There are four primary types. Format specifies the structure of the output, bullet list,

46
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:43.679
numbered steps, table, or prose paragraph. Length specifies the quantity, word count, sentence count,

47
00:04:43.679 --> 00:04:51.679
or a natural boundary like in two paragraphs. Tone specifies the register, formal, informal,

48
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.160
technical, plain language, or a specific stylistic target. Perspective specifies the point of view,

49
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:06.640
first person, third person, or a named role or persona. Each type closes a different category

50
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.959
of interpretation gap. Applying all four to a single instruction isn't over-engineering.

51
00:05:12.959 --> 00:05:17.760
It's closing the four most common sources of output variance in one pass.

52
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:24.480
Not every task needs decomposition, but certain signals tell you when splitting is the right move.

53
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.440
If the task has distinct phases that produce different kinds of output, those phases should

54
00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.760
be separate instructions. If you need to verify one phase before the next phase can run correctly,

55
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.320
decompose. If the instruction requires more than two or three constraints to be even roughly precise,

56
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.799
that's a sign the task is doing too much in one call. And if past attempts with a single prompt

57
00:05:48.799 --> 00:05:54.559
consistently missed part of the task, that's the clearest signal of all. When you find yourself

58
00:05:54.559 --> 00:06:00.239
stacking constraint after constraint to keep a single instruction from going wrong, splitting

59
00:06:00.239 --> 00:06:05.040
is almost always faster and more reliable than trying to make one large prompt airtight.

60
00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:11.679
Testing for specificity means asking structured questions about your instruction before you run it.

61
00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.399
The interpretation test asks, could a reasonable person read this in more than one way? If yes,

62
00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:24.239
find the ambiguity and close it. The gap test asks, what would the model have to assume to

63
00:06:24.239 --> 00:06:30.239
complete this? List those assumptions explicitly, then decide which ones to resolve in the instruction

64
00:06:30.239 --> 00:06:36.239
and which ones are safe to leave implicit. The output preview asks you to mentally trace through

65
00:06:36.239 --> 00:06:41.279
what a plausible response looks like. If two different outputs both seem like reasonable

66
00:06:41.279 --> 00:06:46.720
answers to your instruction, that's the signal you need another constraint. The scope check asks

67
00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:52.079
whether the instruction has a clear stopping point. If it could go on indefinitely, add a boundary.

68
00:06:53.839 --> 00:06:58.720
Picture a complex instruction as a pile of decisions you're handing over all at once.

69
00:06:59.359 --> 00:07:06.079
Decomposition splits that pile into an order you can inspect, and constraints on format, length,

70
00:07:06.079 --> 00:07:11.760
tone, and perspective shrink whatever's left, and you get back what you expect. The whole

71
00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.959
arrangement holds together only if each step produces an output you can actually check,

72
00:07:16.959 --> 00:07:20.799
because an unverifiable step just pushes the guesswork downstream.

73
00:07:21.519 --> 00:07:24.399
That does it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

